  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 214 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and \nsignificantly lower humidity Today-Thursday. Temperatures warm back \nabove normal late in the week an upper ridge builds back into the \nregion. Next chance for rain Fri-Sat.\n\nEarly morning weather analysis shows a strong upper level ridge over \nthe eastern half of the United States with its axis stretching into \nfar northern Canada. With ridge stretching into far northern Canada \nthis allows the 1026mb surface high to move over New England. The \nhigh pressure is in optimal position to allow winds to decouple \ninland and skies to remain clear across the area. The decoupled \nwinds and clear skies is allowing radiational cooling to occur and \nlet temperatures drop into the upper 50s and low 60s inland. \nHowever, along the coast temperatures continue to remain in the low \n70s as winds continue to blow off the warm waters. Through sunrise, \nexpect temps to drop into the low to middle 50s inland and upper 60s \nalong the coast. Through the day high pressure will continue to push \neastward eventually moving offshore. This will allow for a favorable \nearly fall like day across the area. Skies are likely to remain \nmostly clear and temperatures will likely struggle to reach 80 in \nsome areas. Highs for the day are likely to be in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s inland and mid 70s along the coast. By tonight, temperatures \nwill be similar as the previous night with lows in the middle to \nupper 50s inland and low 60s along the coast. \n\nAs the week progresses the high pressure off the New England coast \nwill eventually push SW into the Carolina coastal plain through \nmidweek. This will likely cause high temps to stay mostly in the low-\nmid 80s through Wed with lows in the 50s to lower 60s (locally \nwarmer at the immediate coast). However, dew points are forecasted \nto remain very comfortable through at least Thursday. By the end of \nthe week the upper ridge is progged to expand E-NE to the TN Valley \nand central Appalachians. This will likely lead to above average \ntemps to return to the forecast area  with highs in the upper 80s \nand possibly lower 90s. In addition, rain chances look meager \nthrough much of the week. The best days for rain chances look to be \nFriday and Saturday as a frontal passage approaches the area.",
